"Introducing BigQuery autonomous embedding generation | Google Cloud Blog (https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/data-analytics/introducing-bigquery-autonomous-embedding-generation)\nciteturn29457view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn29456view0\",\"lineno\":25}); Total lines: 172\nL0: cite0†Jump to Content L1: \nL2: cite1†Cloud L3: \nL4: cite2†Blog L5: \nL6: cite3†Contact sales cite4†Get started for free†console.cloud.google.com L7: \nL8: [Input: Find an article...][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input]\nL9: \nL10: cite1†Cloud L11: \nL12: cite2†Blog L13:   * Solutions & technology\nL14:     * cite5†AI & Machine Learning L15:     * cite6†API Management L16:     * cite7†Application Development L17:     * cite8†Application Modernization L18:     * cite9†Chrome Enterprise L19:     * cite10†Compute L20:     * cite11†Containers & Kubernetes L21:     * cite12†Data Analytics L22:     * cite13†Databases L23:     * cite14†DevOps & SRE L24:     * cite15†Maps & Geospatial L25:     * Security\nL26:       * cite16†Security & Identity L27:       * cite17†Threat Intelligence L28:     * cite18†Infrastructure L29:     * cite19†Infrastructure Modernization L30:     * cite20†Networking L31:     * cite21†Productivity & Collaboration L32:     * cite22†SAP on Google Cloud L33:     * cite23†Storage & Data Transfer L34:     * cite24†Sustainability L35:   * Ecosystem\nL36:     * cite25†IT Leaders L37:     * Industries\nL38:       * cite26†Financial Services L39:       * cite27†Healthcare & Life Sciences L40:       * cite28†Manufacturing L41:       * cite29†Media & Entertainment L42:       * cite30†Public Sector L43:       * cite31†Retail L44:       * cite32†Supply Chain L45:       * cite33†Telecommunications L46:     * cite34†Partners L47:     * cite35†Startups & SMB L48:     * cite36†Training & Certifications L49:     * cite37†Inside Google Cloud L50:     * cite38†Google Cloud Next & Events L51:     * cite39†Google Cloud Consulting L52:     * cite40†Google Maps Platform†mapsplatform.google.com L53:     * cite41†Google Workspace†workspace.google.com L54:   * cite42†Developers & Practitioners L55:   * cite25†Transform with Google Cloud L56: cite3†Contact sales cite4†Get started for free†console.cloud.google.com L57: \nL58: [Input: Find an article...][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input]\nL59: \nL60: Data Analytics\nL61: #\nL62: \nL63: Simplify your AI workflow with autonomous embedding generation in BigQuery\nL64: \nL65: February 20, 2026\nL66: \nL67:   *   *   *   * ##### Andong Li\nL68: \nL69: Software Engineer\nL70: \nL71: ##### Brian Seung\nL72: \nL73: Software Engineer\nL74: ##### Try Gemini Enterprise today\nL75: \nL76: The front door to AI in the workplace\nL77: \nL78: cite43†Try now†business.gemini.google L79: In the world of generative AI and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), embeddings are the \"secret sauce\" that allow machines and AI agents to understand the semantic meaning of data. As BigQuery extends its cite44†autonomous data-to-AI platform , embeddings unblock valuable multimodal use cases. However, for many data engineers, managing embeddings is a headache.\nL80: Traditionally, users have to set up embedding generation pipelines themselves to propagate source content updates, embedding generation, and storage.\nL81: To help BigQuery users with their AI workloads, we’re introducing autonomous embedding generation. This feature allows BigQuery to automatically maintain an embedding column on a table based on a source column. No more manual pipelines, no more synchronization issues, just easy, AI-ready data.\nL82: ### Managing embeddings, the old way\nL83: \nL84: Before autonomous generation, the process of updating your vector search database usually looked like this:\nL85: \nL86:   1. Detect new rows in your source table.\nL87: \nL88:   2. Generate embeddings via functions like cite45†AI.EMBED†docs.cloud.google.com .\nL89: \nL90:   3. Handle rate limits and retries.\nL91: \nL92:   4. Update the destination table with the new vectors.\nL93: \nL94:   5. Monitor the progress of your embedding generations.\nL95: If your data changes frequently, keeping these vectors in sync can be a full-time job for the user/administrator. With this as the backdrop, we set out to enhance BigQuery with the following capabilities.\nL96: #### 1. Help the user directly work with their data\nL97: \nL98: We want to simplify the search experience for the user, so that they can do simple things like `AI.SEARCH(TABLE mydataset.products, 'product_description', \"A really fun toy\")`, without having to interact or understand the embeddings.\nL99: \nL100: #### 2. Automatic synchronization\nL101: \nL102: BigQuery should manage embedding generation on behalf of the user and keep generated embeddings in sync with the source data.\nL103: #### 3. Tight integration with vector indexes\nL104: \nL105: BigQuery’s cite46†VECTOR_SEARCH†docs.cloud.google.com has many users, and we want to ensure that the managed embedding was integrated into it.\nL106: ### The solution: autonomously generated embedding columns\nL107: \nL108: We solved this by treating embeddings as a managed part of your table. Using a familiar SQL syntax, you can now define an autonomous embedding column that BigQuery manages for you.\nL109: \nL110: Loading...\nL111: CREATE TABLE mydataset.products ( name STRING, description STRING, description_embedding STRUCT<result ARRAY<FLOAT64>, status STRING> GENERATED ALWAYS AS (AI.EMBED( description, connection_id => 'us.test_connection', endpoint => 'text-embedding-005' )) STORED OPTIONS( asynchronous = TRUE ) );\nL112: \nL113:  For more information, please refer to the cite47†guide†docs.cloud.google.com .\nL114: ### Integration with vector index and vector search\nL115: \nL116: In addition, BigQuery’s cite48†vector index†docs.cloud.google.com and cite46†vector search†docs.cloud.google.com are also integrated with the generated embedding column. You can directly create a vector index associated with the source data column and query your data without managing embeddings manually. BigQuery automatically applies the base table's model to generate compatible embeddings for your query.\nL117: ### Introducing AI.SEARCH\nL118: \nL119: We also launched a new function, cite49†AI.SEARCH†docs.cloud.google.com , to provide a simplified signature for you to get started with the data-centric search experience. AI.SEARCH automatically uses the embedding model associated with the generated embedding column from the base table, so you don’t need to interact with the embedding configuration when using AI.SEARCH or VECTOR_SEARCH.\nL120: \nL121: Loading...\nL122: SELECT base.name, base.description, distance FROM AI.SEARCH(TABLE mydataset.products, 'description', \"A really fun toy\");\nL123: ### Simple management\nL124: \nL125: Autonomous embedding generation is in preview, ready for you to use as part of your data analytics pipelines today. We’ve also invested in a few features to help make the process simpler to manage end to end:\nL126: \nL127:   * Embedding status metadata: You can track the progress of embedding generation by querying the percentage of non-null embeddings in your table:\nL128: \nL129: Loading...\nL130: SELECT COUNTIF(description_embedding IS NOT NULL AND description_embedding.status = '') * 100.0 / COUNT(*) AS percent FROM mydataset.products;\nL131: \nL132: While you can initiate the creation of the vector index at any time, generating an index model will only happen at a scale when performance will benefit.\nL133:   * Native access to Vertex AI models: By ensuring your BigQuery connection has the `Vertex AI User` role, embedding generation can securely \"talk\" to a remote state of the art Vertex AI embedding models on your behalf.\nL134: \nL135:   * Native error monitoring: If any step in the embedding generation pipeline fails, , you can view the status of recent background jobs via INFORMATION_SCHEMA jobs view (cite50†example†docs.cloud.google.com ), Here  you can find detailed error info to help you resolve the issue.\nL136: ### What’s next\nL137: \nL138: Autonomous embedding generation represents a shift toward AI-native multimodal data foundation that’s built for processing and activation of all data types. By automating and coupling embedding generation within the data platform, we’re helping developers spend less time on plumbing and more time on building intelligent applications. And we’re not done yet, and are hard at work building:\nL139: \nL140:   * Simpler connection creation via Data Definition Library (DDL) and Data Control Language (DCL)\nL141:   * The ability to add a generated embedding column to existing tables via ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN DDL\nL142: \nL143:   * API and UI support for managing generated embedding columns\nL144: \nL145:   * Direct support for multimodal data using cite51†ObjectRef†docs.cloud.google.com L146: \nL147: Ready to try it? Check out the cite47†official BigQuery documentation†docs.cloud.google.com to set up your first managed embedding table today.\nL148: \nL149: Posted in\nL150: \nL151:   * cite12†Data Analytics L152:   * cite5†AI & Machine Learning L153:   * cite52†BigQuery L154: ##### Related articles\nL155: \nL156: cite53†Data Analytics Accelerating analytics: PayPal’s journey with Managed Service for Apache Spark By Vinod Ganesan • 3-minute read L157: \nL158: cite54†AI & Machine Learning Empower your agents with the Google Cloud CLI remote MCP server By Prosper Nwankpa • 6-minute read L159: \nL160: cite55†Data Analytics Introducing Ask, a new Google Earth Engine feature to accelerate geospatial coding By Joel Conkling • 4-minute read L161: cite56†Data Analytics Maximizing Apache Spark availability: Mitigating compute stockouts with flexible VMs and other best practices By Sravani Bobbala • 8-minute read L162: ### Footer Links\nL163: \nL164: #### Follow us\nL165: \nL166:   *   *   *   *   *   * cite1†Google Cloud L167:   * cite57†Google Cloud Products L168:   * cite58†Privacy†myaccount.google.com L169:   * cite59†Terms†myaccount.google.com L170:   *   * cite60†Help†support.google.com L171:   * [Select]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBlog | Qwen (https://qwenlm.github.io/blog/)\nciteturn29457view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn29456view1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 37\nL0: ## We have a new blog at cite0†qwen.ai†qwen.ai !\nL1: \nL2: This page will automatically redirect in 5 seconds.\nL3: \nL4: If you are not redirected automatically, please click the button below.\nL5: \nL6: [Button: Go Now]\nL7: \nL8:   * cite1†Blog L9:   * cite2†Publication L10:   * cite3†About L11:   * cite4†Try Qwen Chat †chat.qwen.ai L12: \nL13: # Blog^{  [\nL14: \nL15:   * cite5†简体中文 L16: ]}\nL17: ## Qwen3Guard: Real-time Safety for Your Token Stream\nL18: Tech Report GitHub Hugging Face ModelScope DISCORD Introduction We are excited to introduce Qwen3Guard, the first safety guardrail model in the Qwen family. Built upon the powerful Qwen3 foundation models and fine-tuned specifically for safety classificatoin, Qwen3Guard ensures responsible AI interactions by delivering precise safety detection for both prompts and responses, complete with risk levels and categorized classifications for accurate moderation.\nL19: Qwen3Guard achieves state-of-the-art performance on major safety benchmarks, demonstrating strong capabilities in both prompt and response classification tasks across English, Chinese, and multilingual environments....\nL20: September 23, 2025 · 7 min · 1470 words · Qwen Team\nL21: ## Qwen-Image-Edit: Image Editing with Higher Quality and Efficiency\nL22: QWEN CHAT GITHUB HUGGING FACE MODELSCOPE DISCORD We are excited to introduce Qwen-Image-Edit, the image editing version of Qwen-Image. Built upon our 20B Qwen-Image model, Qwen-Image-Edit successfully extends Qwen-Image’s unique text rendering capabilities to image editing tasks, enabling precise text editing.\nL23: Furthermore, Qwen-Image-Edit simultaneously feeds the input image into Qwen2.5-VL (for visual semantic control) and the VAE Encoder (for visual appearance control), achieving capabilities in both semantic and appearance editing....\nL24: August 19, 2025 · 4 min · 824 words · Qwen Team\nL25: ## Qwen-Image: Crafting with Native Text Rendering\nL26: GITHUB HUGGING FACE MODELSCOPE DEMO DISCORD We are thrilled to release Qwen-Image, a 20B MMDiT image foundation model that achieves significant advances in complex text rendering and precise image editing. To try the latest model, feel free to visit Qwen Chat and choose “Image Generation”. The key features include: Superior Text Rendering: Qwen-Image excels at complex text rendering, including multi-line layouts, paragraph-level semantics, and fine-grained details.\nL27: It supports both alphabetic languages (e....\nL28: August 4, 2025 · 6 min · 1229 words · Qwen Team\nL29: ## GSPO: Towards Scalable Reinforcement Learning for Language Models\nL30: PAPER DISCORD Introduction Reinforcement Learning (RL) has emerged as a pivotal paradigm for scaling language models and enhancing their deep reasoning and problem-solving capabilities. To scale RL, the foremost prerequisite is maintaining stable and robust training dynamics. However, we observe that existing RL algorithms (such as GRPO) exhibit severe instability issues during long training and lead to irreversible model collapse, hindering further performance improvements with increased compute.\nL31: To enable successful RL scaling, we propose the Group Sequence Policy Optimization (GSPO) algorithm....\nL32: July 27, 2025 · 5 min · 916 words · Qwen Team\nL33: ## Qwen-MT: Where Speed Meets Smart Translation\nL34: DEMO API DISCORD Introduction Here we introduce the latest update of Qwen-MT (qwen-mt-turbo) via Qwen API. This update builds upon the powerful Qwen3, leveraging trillions multilingual and translation tokens to comprehensively enhance the model’s multilingual understanding and translation capabilities. By integrating reinforcement learning techniques, the model achieves significant improvements in translation accuracy and linguistic fluency.\nL35: Key Features: Multilingual Support for 92 Languages: Qwen-MT enables high-quality translation across 92 major official languages and prominent dialects, covering over 95% of the global population to meet diverse cross-lingual communication needs....\nL36: July 24, 2025 · 6 min · 1220 words · Qwen Teamcite6†Next  » © 2026 cite7†Qwen Powered by cite8†Hugo†gohugo.io --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAI at Meta Blog (https://ai.meta.com/blog/)\nciteturn29457view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn29456view2\",\"lineno\":45}); Total lines: 270\nL0: #####\nL1: \nL2: ## Products\nL3: \nL4:   * cite0†Muse L5:   * cite1†Meta AI L6:   * cite2†Media Generation L7:   * cite3†Vibes L8: \nL9: #####\nL10: \nL11: ## AI Research\nL12: \nL13:   * cite4†Overview L14:   * cite5†Projects L15:   * cite6†Resources & Tools L16:   * cite7†Publications L17:   * cite8†GitHub†github.com L18: \nL19: #####\nL20: \nL21: ## Resources\nL22: \nL23:   * cite9†Blog L24:   * cite10†Learning Hub L25:   * cite11†Demos†aidemos.meta.com L26: \nL27: #####\nL28: ## About\nL29: \nL30:   * cite12†Overview L31:   * cite13†Open Source L32:   * cite14†Careers†www.metacareers.com L33: \nL34: * cite15†Products L35: * cite15†AI Research L36: * cite15†Resources L37: * cite15†About L38: * cite16†AI Developers†developer.meta.com L39: * cite17†Try Muse†applink.muse.ai L40: * The 